d so an example of a rule that we  often  deal with in paypal is one that captures  a particular type of fraudulent  behaviour um  and this rules logic might look  something like this so if the number of  transactions made from a given email  address in the past four hours is  greater than 10 then reject the uh the  transaction so that's for something like  an e-commerce company  um  now a real base system is essentially  one that leverages  um a number of these rules to provide a  certain outcome  so a one that we tend to deal with is um  a rule-based system that might accept  reject and review transactions for an  e-commerce company  so  um as with kind of any approach um  there's usually pros and cons and um  it's no different for rbs's so the first  pro um for an rbs is that the rules are  intuitive and so the outcome um is  usually quite easy to understand because  um the the rules that have triggered to  give the decision you can usually um  have some intuitive kind of  understanding of um the second prone is  that rbs is quite flexible because you  can add or take away rules and to  address new behavior  and the third pro is that rules are  usually built using domain knowledge um  and that domain knowledge might actually  capture behavior that an ml model  um  would have would have missed  um but in terms of the cons  so the first one is actually linked to  the the last pro in the domain  knowledges is usually required and so to  essentially build rules you usually need  some sort of demand knowledge um in the  first place  and the second con  is that generating these rules can often  be challenging and time consuming  especially if you use a data guided  approach and so it can be very time  consuming to kind of  use that data and manually go through  and try and create rules based on  analysis that um that you're doing  manually  um  and the third con is that it's uh  usually very difficult to tweak existing  rules so um quite often you'll have  existing rules that are already um being  used and if you want to  optimize those um  for new data it's very difficult to do  that  and that's really where iguanas comes in  and helps to  streamline the the process of setting up  an rbs so um the first kind of um  pro to iguanas is that it only requires  a historic data set so it's very similar  to the requirements of a  ml model in that respect and it can also  quickly and easily generate um high  performance rules  um and utilizes an api that's very  familiar to most data scientists and  that's the  the skill sk-learn kind of fit transform  methodology  um  it also has a real optimization module  and that allows users to quickly and  easily um tweak the thresholds of  existing rules in a rule set and based  on new label data  iguanas also has a host of other modules  that really to help streamline the the  obvious setup process and i'll quickly  mention those at the end of the  presentation  so just to give a quick overview of how  the rule generator works in iguanas  so in essence it um  takes a trend tree ensemble model and it  iterates through the branches of each  decision tree with within that model and  extracts the high-performing branches  um and  since the branches are just a set of  conditions they're essentially  rules as well so they can be thought  over as rules  and so there's a very simple example on  the right here and we have a shallow  decision tree and it has two reject leaf  nodes  um and so what the rule generator will  do is just extract those branches  and and output those as reject rules so  in this case the rule number one would  be order amount greater than 500  and a new customer  and then the second rule would be order  amount less than 500  and transaction volume is greater than  20 in the last 90 days  so in terms of uh the types of data you  can apply this to um we  have facilities to work on for it to  work on both pandas and spark data  frames  um and we did a little test on um a data  set of a million rows  um and 150 columns and compared that  with scope which is a  package that is used for real generation  and is relatively well known as well and  we found that it was around four times  faster in that case than than scope as  well which is a good result  so in terms of the the rule optimizer um  so this essentially just takes a set of  existing rules and couples that with a  labeled data set and a and an objective  function and then using version  optimization it will optimize the rules  um and optimize their thresholds and  then output uh  an optimized rule set so  the the cool thing with this is that you  can the user can actually specify um an  exact metric for optimization via that  objective function so it's not limited  to a predefined metric such as precision  you can use any kind of uh  optimization function to to optimize the  rules  um and even though we call it a rule  optimizer um it can actually be applied  to  any condition so for example if you have  a set of rules that work together to  give a decision you can actually use the  rule optimizer to optimize the  thresholds of these rules in tandem  um  and when we tested the rule optimizer on  on a data set again and we found  that and to optimize 50 rules on a dead  set of a million records and it took  around 11 seconds um to run and it also  doubled the mean f1 score of the rules  which was a pretty good result as well  so i mentioned earlier on in the the  presentation that there were additional  modules to help with the setup of an rbs  and so firstly  um we've developed a grid search cv  module and that essentially searches for  the best type of parameters to use when  generating or optimizing rules  and this utilizes uh careful stratified  cross-validation to do that um there's  also a correlation reduction module and  that removes correlated rules uh from a  rule set and given a similarity metric  such as jaccard similarity  the greedy filter  module also removes rules that do not  add value to  or potentially degrade the performance  of an rbs  and then finally we've got the real  scorer module and that generator score  for each rule  and these scores can often be used in  the setup of a an rbs  and  so yeah so many modules  in addition to the real generator and  real optimizer that can help with the  setup of  an rbs  so hopefully you'll be asking at this  point where can you get it um  unfortunately it's it's not currently  available  um just because we're in some final  discussions with the the legal team in  terms of open sourcing it but we have  got  the uh the repo sorted on the uh public  side of the paypal and github um so  that's where it'll be and i'll share  this link in the the slack channel for  pi data  um and then obviously once it's  available we'd really appreciate um  everybody's feedback um comments  questions um and uh yeah it'd be really  great to hear from you guys  um and that wraps it up so thanks again  for for joining us that was useful  okay thank you uh that means we can move  on to the next lightning talk speaker  we're going to talk about pragmatic  advice for implementing responsible  machine learning  uh violeta uh could you set up your  screen please  uh thank you so much for for having me  my name is violeta micheva and i'm  joined by my colleague leonor tidaman  and we are from the foundation for best  practices in machine learning  before i tell you about the foundation  let me just say why  we  started it  basically  most of you would agree that if  developed and deployed carelessly  machine learning can result in harmful  and sometimes detrimental consequences  on the other side we see more and more  institutions coming out with their own  principles and guidelines that machine  learning and ai models should abide by  but most of them remain sort of  very abstract and just lofty principles  it's really not clear how can you  practically implement them  and last but not least it's hard to  operationalize responsible machine  learning without the proper  organizational support  so uh being frustrated by all of this  around 10 or 11 months ago  we founded the foundation for best  practices in machine learning  our mission is to champion ethical and  responsible machine learning through  open source best practices and free  public knowledge  all the foundation members are  volunteers  for none of us this is our main  task so we all do this besides our our  jobs and all of us work in the domain of  ai and machine learning in some way or  another for example  in among our  members we have machine learning  engineers and data scientists  statisticians and researchers but also  legal and compliance professionals ai  addresses etc  so the foundation is a non-for-profit  organization  we create open source best practices and  we are licensed under the creative  commons attribution license  and just to give you a sneak peek of  what the  best practices are  so we've developed so far the so-called  technical best practices and  organizational best practices  you can think of them as two documents  where the technical best practices is  focused on a single product namely a  machine learning model  and the organizational best practices is  focused or its scope is the whole  organization  and the technical best practices are  mediated through life cycle management  they are embedded in the machine  learning development cycle and  organizational best practices are  mediated through policies  and  both of them cover the same subjects  these are areas within  the domain of machine learning which we  as an organization feel like the models  should  should cover in order for them to be  developed and deployed in a responsible  manner  and now i will give the word to my  colleague leonard to tell you a little  bit more on how to get involved if you  are interested in that  indeed so the the technical and the  organizational best practices that are  presented to you they're really the 